<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5l5-3V21M10 21V4h9v17M13 8h3M13 12h3M13 16h3M7.5 13h.01M7.5 16.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>About $640</strong><span class="t">condo fee a month</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5.5 7h8A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 15 8.5v11a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5h-8A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-11A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 5.5 7zM8 7V4.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 9.5 3h9A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 20 4.5v11a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5H15M9.5 17.5l-2.3-2.4a1.4 1.4 0 0 1 2.3-1.6 1.4 1.4 0 0 1 2.3 1.6z"/></svg></span><span><strong>Two layers</strong><span class="t">in some places</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated July 27, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Yes, in law. Under South Carolina's HOA Act, a POA and an HOA are the same kind of group. The kind of home is what changes the fee.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. In South Carolina, a property owners association (POA) and a homeowners association (HOA) are the same thing in law. Any group that runs a planned neighborhood or a condo regime, where a recorded declaration makes owners pay assessments, is a homeowners association under the state's HOA Act, except a timeshare plan. A POA that meets that test is one too.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">What changes the fee is the kind of home. A condo is a horizontal property regime: the owners share the building, the association insures it, and the typical fee is about $640 a month. A house fee is usually about $95. Some communities add a master association and a second charge.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Send us an address. We find every association it belongs to.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us check a property</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#hoa-vs-poa" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">HOA vs POA</a></li><li><a href="#condo-vs-house" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Condo vs house</a></li><li><a href="#what-you-own" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What you own</a></li><li><a href="#regime-fees" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Regime fees</a></li><li><a href="#two-associations" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Two associations</a></li><li><a href="#surprises" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Surprises</a></li><li><a href="#how-we-check" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How we check</a></li><li><a href="#loans" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Loans</a></li><li><a href="#pros-and-cons" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Pros and cons</a></li><li><a href="#hoa-vs-poa-questions" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">HOA vs POA questions</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="hoa-vs-poa"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="HOA vs POA">What is the difference between an HOA and a POA?</h2><style>main#main .hp-pays{max-width:760px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;padding:1.1rem 1.1rem 1rem;border-radius:20px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.8),rgba(255,255,255,.56));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 10px 26px -18px rgba(28,32,40,.35)}main#main .hp-pays .cap{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1.0625rem;line-height:1.4;margin:0 0 .7rem}main#main .hp-pays-t [role=row]{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.25fr) repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.6rem;align-items:center;padding:.5rem 0;border-top:1px so